## 1. Unit discriminant via an exact finite cycle

Fix d prime 7 mod 8, C a native normalized CM lattice, omega=(1+sqrt(-d))/2,
t its specified half-period, T=omega*t. Both T and t-T nonzero mod
Lambda, and distinct from t and each other. The native normalized curve
has e=wp(t), g2=12e^2-4, a1^2=12e. Write
 u=wp(T)-e, v=wp(t-T)-e.
The three distinct roots of the Weierstrass cubic sum to zero and the
derivative at e equals 4. Thus u+v=-3e, uv=1. In particular u,v nonzero,
u!=v, and v^2!=1. Put b=a1^4-64=16(u-v)^2.

For a nonlattice half-period s let S(s)=sum_{lambda in Lambda}(s-lambda)^(-4).
Differentiating wp twice gives S(s)=wp(s)^2-g2/12; hence
 S(t)=1/3, S(T)=(u^2-1)/3, S(t-T)=(v^2-1)/3.
G4=sum over nonzero lambda of lambda^(-4), g2=60 G4.
The exact native invariant V(C)=240G4/S(t-T)-16 therefore equals
 V=16/(v^2*(v^2-1)).

Let D be the normalized lattice in the native class step
I=1+O_K*(-omega/2); its lattice is c*(Lambda union (T+Lambda)).
Indeed the image of omega on Lambda/2Lambda is the order-two
subgroup generated by 2T, by the source mul_mem_or_mul_sub_t_mem,
and T is not a period. c is a nonzero complex scale.
The image of t in this quotient lattice is nonzero and killed by omega.
For D the unique nonzero point killed by omega in D[2] is
(1-omega)t_D: omega(1-omega)=(d+1)/4 even, and the specified
half-period conditions give its nonvanishing and the other direction.
Thus c*t is congruent to t_D-omega*t_D modulo Lambda_D;
c*(t-T) gives the same point. Splitting the absolutely convergent
fourth-power sums into their two disjoint lattice cosets yields
 c^4 G4(D)=G4(C)+S(T),
 c^4 S_D(t_D-omega*t_D)=S(t)+S(t-T)=v^2/3.
Consequently
 W=V(D)=(4g2+240 S(T))/(v^2/3)-16
       =-256*(v^2-1)/v^4,
 W=-b V,                   (48+V+b)*(-W)=4096.          (I)
The two equalities are exact before any norm; no modular polynomial,
no assertion that an arbitrary model has good reduction.

For any prime p and the SAME embedding iota let x=|V|>0,
y=|W|>0, c0=|4096| in (0,1]. Then |b|=y/x and
 |48+V+b|*y=c0, |48|<=1.
Three elementary implications using strict ultrametric dominance:
(a) if x<c0, y>=c0 is impossible: |b|>1 dominates and
c0=y^2/x>y>=c0. Thus y<c0. Since c0/y>1 while |48|,x<=1,
|b|=c0/y and y^2=c0*x>x^2, hence y>x.
(b) if x>1 and y<=x, then |b|<=1 and |V| dominates;
y=c0/x<c0. Otherwise y>x.
(c) if c0<=x<=1 and y>x, |b|>1 dominates,
c0=y^2/x>y>x>=c0, impossible. Thus y<=x.

The ideal class group is finite. The native I class defines a cycle:
choose successive normalized CM lattices by
exists_latticeModule_eq_scaledIdeal_mul and take the final lattice
to be the original C, since I^n principal and
exists_eq_scaledIdeal_mul_of_mk_eq_one. This does NOT require the
intermediate arbitrary scales to be equal or the cycle length bounded.
Every edge has (I). A cyclic sequence cannot visit x<c0 (all further
terms would stay below c0 and increase strictly). It cannot visit x>1
(all further terms increase strictly or fall into the already excluded
region). In the remaining interval [c0,1] it is nonincreasing and
periodic, hence constant. Thus y/x=1 at the original edge.
This proves exactly norm_disc_eq_one, including p=2, with the
native CM lattice normalization and ideal-class/2-isogeny facts.
Finally |a1|^4=|b+64|<=1 gives norm_a1_le_one.

## 2. Quartic doubling: no local cancellation, even at p=2

Write theta and sigma for the native sections, G=phiThetaCube.
The source theta-addition law with equal arguments gives
 theta(2z)=theta(z)^4-sigma(z)^4
using theta(0)=1 and xi(z)theta(z)^2=sigma(z)^2. Also
 sigma(2z)=-wp'(z)*sigma(z)^4 off the lattice.
Set r=wp(z)-e=theta(z)^2/sigma(z)^2. The cubic is
 wp'(z)^2=4*r*(r^2+3e*r+1).
Substitution and exact clearing of sigma gives
 sigma(2z)^2=(sigma(z)*theta(z))^2 *
   [4*(theta(z)^4+sigma(z)^4)+(a1*theta(z)*sigma(z))^2].
Both identities extend everywhere by the identity theorem for entire
functions (the native source does not divide at lattice points).

H(z)=max(|theta(z)|,|sigma(z)|)>0 because their zero sets are
t+Lambda and Lambda, disjoint. Divide theta,sigma by whichever
has largest norm. Write normalized coordinates A,B; both integral,
one a unit. The duplication identities yield H(2z)<=H(z)^4
using |a1|<=1. If strict, reduce modulo the maximal ideal:
 A^4=B^4, (AB)^2*(4*(A^4+B^4)+(a1*A*B)^2)=0.
Both A,B are now units; cancel (AB)^2. Then a1^2*(AB)^2=-8A^4.
Squaring and using (AB)^4=A^8 gives (a1^4-64)A^8=0,
contradiction to the unit discriminant. No division by 2 here.
Therefore H(2z)=H(z)^4 for ALL z.

The homogeneous cubic G^2+a1*sigma^2*theta*G=sigma^6+sigma^2*theta^4
gives |G|<=H^3: divide by the sixth power of the same largest
coordinate. The normalized G/c^3 solves a monic quadratic with
integral coefficients, so if its norm exceeded one, its square
would strictly dominate the other terms. This proves all three
conjuncts of naiveHeight_spec. It also proves
|theta^a sigma^(2b) G^c|<=H^(a+2b+3c) for monomialSection.
By the native section span, each degree-m section F satisfies
|F(z)|<=M_F H(z)^m for some finite fixed M_F, ALL z; choose max of
the finitely many coefficient norms in the spanning expression.

## 3. Tate's trick, using one bounded supremum

Fix the actual isogeny a:C->C' of degree N, torsion y with ny in
Lambda', n>0; let H,H' denote the above local heights.
Write q_y(w)=exp(-Hc'(w,y)-Hc'(y,y)/2), where Hc' is the
complex Hermitian form (not the p-adic height).
Define R_a(z)=|iota(q_y(az))| H'(az+y)/H(z)^N, and
R_1(w) for the identity isogeny of C'. Both functions nonnegative
and bounded uniformly. To prove boundedness, take the theta
section of degree 1 and sigma^2 of degree 2 on C'. The n*2-th
power of the degree-one translated theta and the n-th power of
the degree-two translated sigma^2 are sections: 2n*y is a period
and the native dual-lattice thetaShift_mem_of_dual applies to
degree 2n. Their pullbacks are sections of degree 2nN by
comp_mul_mem_sections. The previous finite span bound, followed
by taking 2n-th roots, bounds |q_y(az)theta'(az+y)|/H(z)^N and
|q_y(az)sigma'(az+y)|/H(z)^N by fixed constants. Their max is R_a.
Apply the same argument to R_1. This is uniform on all complex
z, not only an archimedean fundamental domain.

The exact translation-factor cancellation
 q_y(w)^4=q_y(2w)*q_y(2w+y)
follows by adding the two linear exponents. Quartic height
doubling then yields, before taking any supremum,
 R_a(z)^4=R_a(2z)*R_1(2az+y).
The same formula holds for R_1 with a=1. Put
 M=max(1,sup_z R_a(z),sup_w R_1(w)).
It is finite and >=1; both identities imply M^4<=M^2, so M=1.
Consequently for any g of the native degree m satisfying the
pointwise hypothesis |g(w)|<=H'(w)^m,
 |(U_y^m g)(az)| <= (|q_y(az)| H'(az+y))^m <= H(z)^(mN).
This is exactly norm_translate_le_naiveHeight_pow. No extra
height constant, asymptotic limit or additional integrality premise.
For the actual isogeny/translation consumer in IsogenyIntegrality,
combine with RUN57's section integrality using the same C,p,iota,
degree, and torsion point. Its normalized residual is integral.

## 4. Formal coordinate ring closure

The translated coordinates X=xi, Y=phi satisfy
 Y^2+a1*X*Y=X^3+X, X(0)=Y(0)=0, Y'(0)=1.
Express X as a series in Y (invert the linear coefficient of Y).
The exact identity X=Y^2+a1*X*Y-X^3 determines each coefficient
of X recursively from earlier coefficients; since a1 integral,
all are integral. The formal implicit solution is unique
(coefficientwise), giving taylorVia_translatedWp_mem_phiSeriesRing.
Also Y'=1+3X^2-a1*Y is in that ring. For any integral f(Y),
d/dz f(Y)=f'(Y)Y' has integral coefficients; derivatives introduce
only integer multipliers. This supplies deriv_mem_phiSeriesRing
without a separate integrality hypothesis. The ordinary formal
Taylor chain rule, native differential equation, and normalization
are retained.

